        "id": 8536,
        "name": "dpt",
        "username": "dpt",
        "avatar_template": "/user_avatar/forums.greaterthangames.com/dpt/{size}/1922_2.png",
        "created_at": "2013-08-15T14:54:10.000Z",
        "cooked": "<p>I haven't had a chance to play Nightmist yet, but looking through her cards I'm guessing that it would be helpful to keep track of the card values, to, for instance, know how badly you will screw your allies with Oblivion. &nbsp;The spell values are</p><ul>\n<li>8 cards with spell value 1,</li>\n<li>11 cards with spell value 2,</li>\n<li>13 cards with spell value 3, and</li>\n<li>8 cards with spell value 4.</li>\n</ul><p>One way to keep track of the average value of her deck is as follows:</p><p>Start with a count of 2. &nbsp;<span>Every time you </span><span>draw a card (including your initial hand, naturally), modify the count as follows:</span></p><ul>\n<li><span>Spell value 1: add 3</span></li>\n<li><span>Spell value 2: add 1</span></li>\n<li><span>Spell value 3: subtract 1</span></li>\n<li><span>Spell value 4: subtract 3</span></li>\n</ul><p><span>​The average value of the next card drawn is 2.5 + (count)/2*(cards remaining). &nbsp;Naturally I don't expect to actually do that division; just, if the count is high and you have few cards left, you know some big numbers are coming.</span></p><p><span>I'm not yet sure how useful this will actually be in practice.</span></p>",
        "post_number": 1,
        "post_type": 1,
        "updated_at": "2021-02-07T01:39:05.558Z",
