        "raw": "<p>I haven&#39;t had a chance to play Nightmist yet, but looking through her cards I&#39;m guessing that it would be helpful to keep track of the card values, to, for instance, know how badly you will screw your allies with Oblivion. &nbsp;The spell values are</p><ul><li>8 cards with spell value 1,</li><li>11 cards with spell value 2,</li><li>13 cards with spell value 3, and</li><li>8 cards with spell value 4.</li></ul><p>One way to keep track of the average value of her deck is as follows:</p><p>Start with a count of 2. &nbsp;<span style=\"font-size: 13px;\">Every time you </span><span style=\"font-size: 13px;\">draw a card (including your initial hand, naturally), modify the count as follows:</span></p><ul><li><span style=\"font-size: 13px;\">Spell value 1: add 3</span></li><li><span style=\"font-size: 13px;\">Spell value 2: add 1</span></li><li><span style=\"font-size: 13px;\">Spell value 3: subtract 1</span></li><li><span style=\"font-size: 13px;\">Spell value 4: subtract 3</span></li></ul><p><span style=\"font-size: 13px;\">​The average value of the next card drawn is 2.5 + (count)/2*(cards remaining). &nbsp;Naturally I don&#39;t expect to actually do that division; just, if the count is high and you have few cards left, you know some big numbers are coming.</span></p><p><span style=\"font-size: 13px;\">I&#39;m not yet sure how useful this will actually be in practice.</span></p>",
